# ---- activity 1 ----
sub("A second approver can be enabled as a setting and is off by default. ", "If the sponsor requires a second approver, it is added as rows of the table. ")
para("The activity is accepted at a design review against a checklist:", "In the first month a small end-to-end prototype (import of a substitute file, BM25 search, display of the results) checks the chosen libraries and the environment before the full build. The activity is accepted at a design review against a checklist: every function in scope maps to a component, an interface and a test; the transition table enumerates every state, event and role used by the approval tests; and every open owner input is listed with the default applied. If sponsor inputs on the export format, assessment content, approval model and accounts do not arrive, the substitute schema and defaults keep the build moving, and a later change is a configuration change, not a redesign (Section 17). The specification also fixes the funded function set, so that an optional function can enter the build only through a recorded owner decision.")

# ---- activity 2 ----
para("The protocol and judging guide fix the grade scales", "The protocol and judging guide fix the grade scales with worked examples; the three configurations; the metrics, namely nDCG@10 (Järvelin and Kekäläinen, 2002) for similar submissions, P@5 at grade 2 or above for solution examples and success@10 for external prior art, with Recall@20, MRR, bpref and judged@10 as secondary and completeness measures; the planning values and their confirmation status; the decision rule; and the acceptance targets. The protocol records its freeze date. Each requirement has one pre-specified primary comparison: the hybrid configuration against BM25 for similar submissions and, on each registered source, for solution examples; edited automatic queries against unaided keyword queries for external prior art. The result is the mean paired difference in the primary metric with a two-sided 95% paired bootstrap interval, and a paired randomization test p-value is reported beside it. For the two ranking tasks the margin is 0.05, a planning value: the hybrid configuration is described as not worse than BM25 by more than the margin only if the lower bound of the interval lies above minus the margin, as better only if the lower bound lies above zero, as worse only if the upper bound lies below minus the margin, and otherwise as inconclusive. Reading the interval first against the margin and then against zero follows the closed-testing argument of the CPMP points to consider on switching between superiority and non-inferiority (CPMP, 2000). The EMA page for that document states that it is superseded by the guideline on non-inferiority and equivalence comparisons in clinical trials, but that guideline (EMA/301654/2025) was published as a draft for consultation until 31 May 2026 and its page, checked on 21 November 2026, gives no adopted version or legal effective date; the interval criterion is checked against the adopted text, if any, before the protocol is frozen. The external prior-art comparison is reported as an estimate with its interval and no inferential claim. Each primary comparison answers a different requirement and is reported on its own; dense-only results and secondary metrics are descriptive. Absence of relevant items is stated only at the level checked: the judged pool or a deeper sampled check. Bpref is reported because standard measures become unreliable when relevance judgments are substantially incomplete (Buckley and Voorhees, 2004).")
para("Test collections are built per track", "Test collections are built per track with time-ordered development and test splits. Each collection carries its source, licence note, track label, split dates and checksum, and a manifest script rejects any collection that mixes tracks. If no solution-example source is confirmed and the owner approves it, a substitute is constructed for solution-example retrieval: project judges write 50 need statements in the form of a submission, which are searched against a public solution-description source whose terms pass the licence check. Thirty of the same statements serve as the public need statements for external prior-art evaluation, so one set is written for both tasks. Without the substitute, the solution-example target could not be tested on any data.")
para("The harness reads run files in the standard six-column format,", "The harness reads run files in the standard six-column format, computes the metrics through the toolkit's evaluation functions and through project code for success@10 and judged@10, and implements, with established statistical libraries where they provide the procedure, the paired bootstrap, the sign-flip randomization test, Clopper–Pearson intervals for single proportions such as the \"no example found\" rate (Clopper and Pearson, 1934) and, for the paired binary outcome of external prior art, a score-type interval for paired proportions fixed in the protocol. The randomization and bootstrap tests are used because, in a comparison on TREC ad-hoc runs, they agreed closely with the t-test while the Wilcoxon and sign tests were less able to detect significance and could produce false detections (Smucker et al., 2007). Metric code is unit-tested against hand-computed examples. A judging screen shows one query and one item at a time, in random order, without the name of the configuration that retrieved the item, and records the grade and the time taken. The harness is accepted when it reproduces a published BM25 reference run within rounding; a harness that cannot reproduce a published figure is not used for any comparison. The frozen protocol, the time-ordered split and the single test run keep tuning out of the test results; pooling all three configurations limits the bias against a configuration that retrieves items the others miss.")

# ---- activity 3 ----
para("For each registered collection the index layer", "For each registered collection the index layer builds one lexical index over passages that carry their record's title and one vector index over L2-normalized passage embeddings from the dense encoder, and stores the index version with every query record. The retrieval service runs three configurations behind one interface: BM25 (Robertson and Zaragoza, 2009); the dense encoder without fine-tuning, since no labelled training pairs exist; and reciprocal rank fusion of the two (Cormack et al., 2009). The fingerprint baseline flags exact and near-identical submissions alongside the ranked list. A record's score is its highest passage score, and that passage is returned as the evidence shown before the score. A similar-submission query searches only items dated before the submission. For vector search this is done by filtered search if the pinned Faiss version supports it, and otherwise by retrieving extra candidates and filtering them, with the number of candidates lost to filtering logged. The prototype and the evaluation use exact vector search; at the corpus sizes tested, approximate search is not needed.")
para("The display threshold for similar submissions", "The display threshold for similar submissions and the \"no example above threshold\" signal for solution examples are set on the development split only, by a rule fixed in the protocol, and are not changed after the test run. Encoding and search times are measured on the reference environment at 10,000 and 100,000 items, the planning sizes; a configuration that cannot meet the response-time target of 5 seconds at the 95th percentile, a planning value, is reported as such and is not used as the default. Larger collections are outside the tested range and would need approximate search or index compression. Unit and integration tests cover parsing, validation, hashing, segmentation, index building, date filtering and the presence of an evidence passage for every candidate. If the sponsor releases an export, it is parsed against the field map as a feasibility test.")
sub("(256 word pieces for all-MiniLM-L6-v2, 512 tokens for multilingual-e5-base)", "(512 tokens for multilingual-e5-base)")
sub("The activity is accepted when every configuration that passed the timing check produces a run file on the development split,", "The activity is accepted when each of the three configurations produces a run file on the development split,")

# ---- activity 4 ----
sub("The evaluation compares three formulations for each need statement: an unaided keyword query written by a judge who has not seen the automatic query, the automatic query, and the automatic query after a different judge, acting in the assessor role, has edited it at the approval step. The edited query is the confirmatory formulation; the unedited automatic query is reported as exploratory.", "The evaluation compares two formulations for each need statement: an unaided keyword query written by a judge who has not seen the automatic query, and the automatic query after a different judge, acting in the assessor role, has edited it at the approval step.")

